  "claim": "This paper provides evidence of the existence of intraday momentum strategies, specifically that the first half-hour return can predict the last half-hour return for S&P500 and CSI300 indices, especially during the COVID-19 pandemic, and that these patterns are more robust on days with high volatility and volume.",
  "universe": "The study investigates intraday momentum for the S&P500 index (USA market) and the CSI300 index (China market). Data is from January 1, 2020, to September 11, 2020, a period affected by COVID-19.",
  "horizon": {
    "value": "intraday",
    "note": "The paper focuses on predicting the last half-hour return of a trading day based on earlier half-hour returns (first or second-to-last) within the same day."
  },
  "signal_construction": {
    "value": "The signal is constructed using Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) regression models to predict the last half-hour return (IMreturn13 for S&P500, IMreturn8 for CSI300) based on the first half-hour return (IMreturn1) and/or the second-to-last half-hour return (IMreturn12 for S&P500, IMreturn7 for CSI300). Returns are calculated as the price at the current half-hour divided by the previous half-hour price. Trading strategies involve taking a long position if the first half-hour signal (IMreturn1) is positive, otherwise closing the market each trading day. When using both IMreturn1 and IMreturn12 (or IMreturn7 for CSI300), if all returns are positive, a long position is taken, otherwise a short position. The paper also mentions 'Always long strategies' and 'Buy-and-hold strategies' as general approaches."
  },
